  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The next chance of showers and thunderstorms will\ntake place for Friday through the upcoming weekend. Afterward, \nhot and humid conditions should arrive for the following week.\n\nWhile high pressure remains in control for today and Thursday to\nkeep conditions dry, a cold front will organize across the\ncentral Plains and approach the Appalachian Mountains later on\nFriday. The chance of showers and thunderstorms should increase\nby Friday afternoon and evening. However, the frontal boundary \nshould become parallel with the zonal flow aloft during the \nweekend to mark a continuing stretch of unsettled weather with \ndaily chances of showers and thunderstorms. Due to ample \ninstability and moisture present, there is an opportunity for \nstrong storms that could produce gusty winds and heavy \ndownpours. Although drought conditions still remain in place \nacross the region, any storms training over the same locations \nmay present hydrological issues.\n\nSome of the models indicate that the frontal boundary could exit\noffshore by Monday. A large upper level ridge may build across\nthe Mississippi River Valley for the early part of next week. \nThis synoptic pattern would provide a somewhat drier trend with \nany coverage of showers and storms being limited to terrain and \ndiurnal heating. More importantly, the biggest impact from this \npattern will be the increase in heat and humidity. Temperatures \nmay reach the upper 80s to the upper 90s towards the middle of \nnext week, and heat indices could possibly enter the lower 100s \nacross the Piedmont. Hot and humid weather could last through \nthe remainder of the week.",
